<p class="gna-lede">A white patch in the mouth that does not wipe off and has no obvious cause may be leukoplakia — a potentially malignant disorder, which means it carries a higher-than-normal risk of turning into mouth cancer over time, even though most leukoplakias never do. Because the risky patches cannot be picked out by eye, any white or red patch that is still present two weeks after removing an obvious irritant should be examined by a specialist, and many will need a small biopsy.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-leukoplakia">What is leukoplakia?</h2>
<p>Leukoplakia simply means "white patch". Doctors use the term for a white plaque on the lining of the mouth that cannot be rubbed off and cannot be explained by another condition such as thrush, cheek biting or friction from a sharp tooth. In other words, it is partly a diagnosis of exclusion: the other causes are ruled out first.</p>
<p>The World Health Organization groups leukoplakia with a set of <strong>oral potentially malignant disorders</strong> — conditions in which the lining of the mouth has a raised chance of developing cancer. Others in this group include erythroplakia (red patches), oral submucous fibrosis and oral lichen planus. "Potentially malignant" does not mean cancer. It means the patch needs to be assessed, sometimes biopsied, and then watched.</p>
<p>Leukoplakia usually causes no pain or discomfort, which is exactly why it is so often ignored. It can appear on the inside of the cheeks, the gums, the tongue, the floor of the mouth or the palate. Patches on the floor of the mouth and on the sides and underside of the tongue are generally considered higher-risk sites.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Leukoplakia is a white patch that does not rub off and has no other explanation.</li>
    <li>It is a potentially malignant disorder: most never become cancer, but a minority do, sometimes years later.</li>
    <li>Tobacco in any form — smoked or chewed, including gutka, khaini and zarda — and areca nut are major causes in India.</li>
    <li>Red, or speckled red-and-white, patches carry a higher risk than plain white ones.</li>
    <li>Any patch or ulcer still present after two weeks needs a specialist examination; a biopsy is the only reliable way to judge the risk.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="causes-and-risk">What causes it? Tobacco, gutka and areca nut</h2>
<p>In India, the strongest links are with tobacco and areca nut. The risk rises with how much, how often and for how long they are used, and it is highest where the product is held against the same spot for hours each day.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Smokeless tobacco</strong> — gutka, khaini, zarda, mawa, tobacco-containing pan masala and betel quid with tobacco. The patch often appears exactly where the quid is kept, typically in the cheek or the groove between cheek and gum.</li>
  <li><strong>Areca nut (supari)</strong>, even without tobacco. Areca nut is classed as a cause of cancer in humans and is also the main cause of oral submucous fibrosis.</li>
  <li><strong>Smoking</strong> cigarettes or bidis.</li>
  <li><strong>Alcohol</strong>, which multiplies the harmful effect of tobacco.</li>
</ul>
<p>Some leukoplakias appear in people who have never used tobacco or areca nut. These are sometimes called idiopathic leukoplakias, and they should not be assumed to be safer — some studies suggest they may carry a higher risk of change than tobacco-related patches.</p>
<p>The encouraging point is that stopping helps. Some tobacco-related white patches shrink or disappear within weeks to months of quitting, and quitting lowers the chance of new ones. For support, ask your doctor about tobacco cessation help or call the national quitline number printed on tobacco packets.</p>

<h2 id="should-you-worry">Should you worry? How doctors judge the risk</h2>
<p>Most leukoplakias never turn into cancer. Published figures vary widely, but overall a small minority do so over a period of years. The difficulty is that nobody can tell by eye which patch will change, so specialists combine what they see and feel with the biopsy result.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Lower concern</th><th>Higher concern</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Appearance</td><td>Flat, thin and uniformly white (homogeneous)</td><td>Speckled red and white, lumpy, warty or ulcerated (non-homogeneous)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Site</td><td>Areas other than those listed opposite</td><td>Sides or underside of the tongue, floor of the mouth</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Size and number</td><td>Small, single patch</td><td>Large patch, or several patches</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Biopsy</td><td>No dysplasia (no abnormal cells)</td><td>Moderate or severe dysplasia</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Behaviour</td><td>Stable, or shrinking after quitting habits</td><td>Growing, thickening, bleeding or becoming sore</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<h3>Proliferative verrucous leukoplakia</h3>
<p>A less common form, proliferative verrucous leukoplakia (PVL), spreads slowly to several areas of the mouth, becomes thick and warty, and tends to come back after treatment. It is seen more often in older women and in people who have never used tobacco. It carries a high risk of change and needs close, long-term specialist follow-up.</p>
<h3>Red patches: erythroplakia</h3>
<p>A red, velvety patch that cannot be explained by injury or infection is called erythroplakia. It is much less common than leukoplakia but considerably more serious: a large proportion show severe dysplasia or early cancer when biopsied. Any unexplained red patch, or a white patch with red areas within it (erythroleukoplakia), should be seen by a specialist without delay.</p>

<h2 id="look-alikes">White patches that are not leukoplakia</h2>
<p>Many white patches have harmless explanations, which is why an examination comes before any label:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Typical features</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Oral thrush (candidiasis)</td><td>Creamy white patches that wipe off, leaving a red, sometimes sore surface; common after antibiotics, with dentures, inhaled steroids or diabetes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Frictional keratosis</td><td>A white area next to a sharp tooth, broken filling or ill-fitting denture; fades once the cause is fixed</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Cheek or tongue biting</td><td>Shaggy white patches along the bite line, often on both sides</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Oral lichen planus</td><td>Lacy white lines, often on both cheeks, sometimes with red or sore areas; needs monitoring in its own right</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Leukoedema</td><td>A filmy, greyish-white sheen on the cheeks that fades when the cheek is stretched; harmless</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Oral submucous fibrosis</td><td>Marble-like pallor, stiff cheeks and reduced mouth opening in areca nut users</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Lichen planus and submucous fibrosis also belong to the potentially malignant group. Our guide to <a href="/blogs/oral-submucous-fibrosis-causes-symptoms-treatment/">oral submucous fibrosis</a> covers that condition in detail.</p>

