<h2 id="two-week-rule">The two-week rule: when to see a specialist</h2>
<p>A practical rule used by many oral surgeons: if an ulcer, white patch, red patch or lump in the mouth is still there <strong>two weeks</strong> after any obvious cause has been dealt with — a sharp tooth smoothed, a denture adjusted, an infection treated — it should be examined by a specialist. The NHS similarly advises seeing a doctor or dentist about any mouth ulcer that has not healed within three weeks. Do not wait longer than that, and do not rely on repeated courses of gels, mouthwashes or antibiotics without a diagnosis.</p>
<div class="gna-callout gna-callout--warn">
  <p><strong>Seek a prompt specialist opinion if</strong> you notice a red or speckled patch; a patch that is thickening, bleeding or becoming painful; an ulcer that will not heal; a lump in the mouth or neck; numbness of the tongue or lip; teeth loosening without gum disease; or difficulty swallowing or opening the mouth. These do not always mean cancer, but they need to be checked quickly. Read more about the <a href="/blogs/what-are-the-symptoms-of-oral-cancer/">symptoms of oral cancer</a>.</p>
</div>
<p>If you use tobacco or areca nut in any form, a regular mouth check by your dentist or an oral and maxillofacial surgeon is worthwhile even when you have no symptoms. Our article on <a href="/blogs/early-detection-in-oral-cancers/">early detection of oral cancer</a> explains why timing matters so much.</p>

<h2 id="biopsy">How it is diagnosed: examination and biopsy</h2>
<p>At a consultation, the surgeon asks about tobacco, areca nut and alcohol use, examines the whole mouth and neck in good light, and feels the patch and the lymph nodes in the neck. If a local irritant seems likely, it is removed first and the patch is reviewed after about two weeks.</p>
<p>If the patch persists, or looks high-risk from the start, a <strong>biopsy</strong> is the next step. Under local anaesthetic, a small piece of tissue — usually a few millimetres across — is taken from the most suspicious area, and the wound is closed with one or two dissolvable stitches. The procedure typically takes 15 to 20 minutes. A pathologist then examines the tissue under the microscope and reports whether there is <strong>dysplasia</strong> (abnormal cells), graded as mild, moderate or severe, or whether cancer is present. Large or multiple patches may need samples from several areas.</p>
<p>Special dyes, light-based screening devices and brush samples can help decide where to biopsy, but none of them replaces a biopsy for diagnosis.</p>

<h2 id="treatment-follow-up">Treatment, recovery and follow-up</h2>
<h3>Stopping the cause</h3>
<p>Stopping tobacco, areca nut and alcohol is the foundation of treatment, whatever else is done. It may shrink the patch and lowers the chance of new ones appearing.</p>
<h3>Removing the patch</h3>
<p>Depending on the biopsy result, size and site, the surgeon may recommend removing the patch completely, with a scalpel or a laser. Patches with moderate or severe dysplasia are usually removed; for small patches without dysplasia, careful monitoring can be a reasonable alternative. Removal allows the whole lesion to be examined, which occasionally reveals more advanced change than the first biopsy showed. If a biopsy does show cancer, our guide to <a href="/blogs/oral-cancer-surgery-and-reconstruction-guide/">oral cancer surgery and reconstruction</a> explains what usually happens next.</p>
<h3>Recovery and risks</h3>
<p>After a biopsy or small excision, expect soreness for a few days, eased by simple painkillers, soft food and salt-water rinses. Larger excisions take two to three weeks to heal, can leave some tightness or scarring, and occasionally need a graft or local flap to cover the area. Bleeding and infection are uncommon. It is important to understand that removal does not eliminate the risk: leukoplakia can recur at the same site, new patches can appear elsewhere, and no treatment has been proven to prevent cancer completely. Medicines such as vitamin A derivatives have been studied, but the evidence that they prevent cancer is weak.</p>
<h3>Long-term follow-up</h3>
<p>Whether or not the patch is removed, regular reviews matter — often every three to six months at first, then less frequently if things remain stable. Report any new patch, ulcer or change straight away rather than waiting for your next appointment. At Gnathos Facial in Gachibowli, Hyderabad, our <a href="/oral-cancer">oral cancer screening and treatment</a> service covers examination, biopsy, removal and long-term follow-up.</p>
